    "perplexity": {
      "description": "Homegrown is Dodgy’s second studio album, released in 1994 on A&M Records and widely regarded as the record that gave the band its breakthrough. It builds on the group’s melodic indie-rock and early Britpop sound, pairing bright guitar lines and tight harmonies with an easy, upbeat feel that made songs like “Staying Out for the Summer” and “So Let Me Go Far” stand out as strong singles.\n\nThe album was produced by Hugh Jones and Ian Broudie, and it reached No. 28 on the UK Albums Chart while later earning gold certification in the UK. Critics and listeners often noted its sunny, retro-leaning character, with influences that drew on 1960s guitar pop and a distinctly optimistic, singalong energy; even the album’s marketing leaned into that playful image, with some promotional copies including cannabis seeds.",
